INT. ICE RINK RESURFACER GARAGE - 3 A.M.

A concrete bay. Fluorescent tubes. A Zamboni steaming in the corner. A cracked radio on a shelf plays a farm report.

COACH DOLORES, 50s, in a stained team jacket, sits on an overturned bucket with a clipboard. RAFFERTY, 30s, the goalie, taping his stick, doesn't look up. KWAME, 20s, a rookie forward, hunches over a protein shake. TULLY, 40s, the driver, polishes the Zamboni's blade with a rag.

DOLORES
(to the room)
We lost by seven.

Nobody answers.

DOLORES (CONT'D)
(to Rafferty)
You let in five.

RAFFERTY
Four. One was an own goal.

DOLORES
Who was it?

RAFFERTY
Kwame.

KWAME
It hit my skate!

RAFFERTY
It hit the net after it hit your skate.

KWAME
That's not my fault!

DOLORES
Kwame. Sit.

Kwame sits. The shake slops. Tully tosses him a rag. He catches it. Wipes.

TULLY
You can pay me back in wins.

KWAME
When?

TULLY
When you stop scoring on yourself.

Rafferty snorts. Kwame looks at him. They both grin. Dolores glances up from the clipboard.

DOLORES
That's the first smile I've seen since February.

RAFFERTY
We've been losing since February.

DOLORES
(to herself)
Since October, really.

TULLY
Coach, is there a game tomorrow?

DOLORES
There's a game every day, Tully.

TULLY
Is there a win?

DOLORES
Not yet.

She sets down the clipboard. Stands. Her knees crack. She crosses to the Zamboni, runs a hand along its flank.

DOLORES (CONT'D)
(to the whole garage)
Listen. Nobody's losing sleep tonight. Go home.

RAFFERTY
You first, Coach.

DOLORES
I'm not tired.

TULLY
You're sitting on a bucket.

DOLORES
Best seat in the building.

Kwame stands, stretches, claps Rafferty's shoulder. Rafferty swats his hand away. They bump chests, hard, then lean into each other and shove, laughing, until Tully swings the rag between them like a referee's flag and Dolores, watching all three, shakes her head.

DOLORES (CONT'D)
(softly)
Idiots.

Tully flicks the Zamboni's engine. It coughs. Steam fills the garage.
